# Image editor

This editor adjusts brightness, contrast, saturation and hue while keeping the crop, preview and exported pixels consistent. It replaces the background with a drawn canvas through typed snippets.

<Example source={ImageEditorSource} title="ImageEditor" files={{ 'AdjustableCropperBackground.svelte': ImageEditorFile0, 'AdjustableImage.svelte': ImageEditorFile1, 'AdjustablePreviewBackground.svelte': ImageEditorFile2, 'Button.svelte': ImageEditorFile3, 'Navigation.svelte': ImageEditorFile4, 'Slider.svelte': ImageEditorFile5, 'filters.ts': ImageEditorFile6 }}>
	<ImageEditor />
</Example>

## Keep adjustments reactive

The editor stores the adjustment values in `$state`. Each slider binds to the selected adjustment. A derived crop-mode flag enables image movement and scaling and the child stencil's controls only when cropping is active.

Changing an adjustment redraws the canvas without changing `src` or resetting the user's crop. Uploading a different image resets the adjustment controls and releases the previous owned object URL.

## Draw the source

`AdjustableImage` loads the photo through `CropperSource` with `bind:element`, then draws it into a canvas. An effect tracks the adjustment values; the source's load event triggers the first draw. The filter helper uses native canvas filters when supported and a pixel-processing fallback otherwise.

<CodeBlock html={imageSource.html} code={imageSource.code} />

<CodeBlock html={filtersSource.html} code={filtersSource.code} />

## Register the filtered export source

`CropperBackgroundSnippetProps` supplies `cropper`, `crossOrigin`, `class`, `style` and `attachSource`. The background computes image placement with `getBackgroundStyle` and forwards those values to `AdjustableImage`.

<CodeBlock html={backgroundSource.html} code={backgroundSource.code} />

The actual canvas carries `{@attach attachSource?.(readiness.ready)}`. Each image generation owns a readiness promise, resolved after its first complete filtered draw. With canvas enabled, cropper `onReady` waits for that source. Without a current ready source, `getCanvas()` returns `null`; it does not silently export the unfiltered photo.

For a production background with asynchronous drawing, reject readiness when loading or drawing fails and surface the error. The demo shows the successful drawing path. Registration cleanup belongs to the attachment; an old canvas cannot unregister its replacement.

## Render a matching preview

The preview's `background` snippet receives a read-only `preview` source and its fitted `size`. `getPreviewStyle` positions the same adjusted image within that space. It needs no export-source attachment because previews do not export pixels.

<CodeBlock html={previewSource.html} code={previewSource.code} />

Passing the bound cropper to `CropperPreview` keeps geometry reactive. Passing the same adjustments to both backgrounds keeps the pixels consistent. The download action calls `getCanvas()` on the cropper, so its result comes from the registered filtered canvas.

For bounded Blob encoding and error handling, combine this approach with [Preview and export](/docs/guides/preview-and-export#blob-export-and-memory).
